From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: init host index before
 hash-mode add

ppe_host_pool_get_and_ref() declares `u32 idx;` uninitialized and
passes &idx straight into ppe_host_entry_add(), which for
PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_HASH ends up FIELD_PREP()'d into the OP command
register's ENTRY_INDEX bits (ppe_host_tbl_op_wait()) even though the
function's own doc comment says hardware ignores that field on input
in HASH mode and only sets it on output. Relying on that being true
regardless of whatever garbage is on the stack is reading from
uninitialized memory to build a hardware command word -- undefined
behavior in C regardless of whether this specific field happens to be
truly don't-care on this silicon, and worth fixing on its own merits.

ppe_flow_entry_add()'s caller (ppe_offload_flow_replace()) already
does the equivalent (`flow_index = 0;`) before its own HASH-mode add;
this brings the host-table path in line with that existing convention.

Found while instrumenting a live host-table ADD timeout
(project_flint3_ppe_hw_offload_research.md) -- in the specific runs
observed, the resulting ENTRY_INDEX bits in the readback OP register
were already zero, so this was not the cause of that particular
timeout, but it's a real defect independent of that investigation and
is fixed here regardless of its outcome.

Compile-tested (clean module build, drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe
rebuilt standalone against the existing kernel build tree).

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_host_pool.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_host_pool.c
@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@
 			       unsigned int num_words, u32 *index)
 {
 	struct ppe_host_pool_entry *e;
-	u32 idx;
+	u32 idx = 0;
 	int ret;

 	ret = ppe_host_entry_add(ppe_dev, mode, entry, num_words, &idx);
